 Este estudio propone un modelo de fachada cinética para el equilibrio térmico y la optimización de la luz natural. Inspirado en la base de datos de AskNature, imita los micropelos de la hormiga plateada del Sahara para reflejar el calor, el mecanismo de movimiento del musgo español y la geometría del panal. Así, ofrece una solución sostenible de termorregulación para envolventes arquitectónicas modernas",biomimética; diseño inspirado en la naturaleza; fachada cinética; fachada receptiva; termorregulación · Öznur KARAKUŞ · Semra ARSLAN-SELÇUK · Güneş MUTLU-AVİNÇ (Bogotá) VOL 28 · Nº 2 · JULIO-DICIEMBRE 2026 · ISSN: 1657-0308 · E-ISSN: 2357-626X | 37,marcadores,1.0.0,03d654ba2004f6d3
2357-626X,3-6931-Residenando+aldeas_2026.pdf,3-6931-Residenando+aldeas_2026_0,es,"ta (Google Maps, planos, fotografías) y referentes internacionales","Cómo citar | 55 REVISTA DE (BOGOTÁ)56 | Abstract * Arquitecta, Universidad Nacional de Ingeniería. Lima (Perú). Filiación institucional: Universidad Nacional de Ingeniería, Facultad de Arquitectura, Urbanismo y Artes. Lima (Perú). https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=es&authuser=1&user=ysHX0usAAAAJ https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2748-8627 fmechatol@uni.pe / fiorella0301@gmail.com Thousands of children and adolescents in situations of abandonment live in infrastructures that, while functional, do not promote their overall well-being or emotional development. In this context, the present article reflects on the architecture of children’s villages and similar typologies in Peru, proposing a typological reconfiguration with an architectural-landscape approach grounded in pedagogical principles. The aim is to identify design criteria that transform these spaces into educational, stimulating, and well-being-oriented environments. The literature review encompassed approaches such as neuroarchitecture, Montessori, Waldorf, and Patio Vivo pedagogies, as well as national and international regulatory guidelines related to the typology under study. The research adopted a qualitative exploratory approach, based on the analysis of multiple cases, combining indirect observation (Google Maps, plans, photographs) with international references in Europe, Asia, and Latin America. The study comprised three phases: diagnosis of Peruvian children’s villages, review of global references, and formulation of spatial guidelines. Among the findings, an underuse of outdoor areas, limited diversity of uses, and poor landscape integration were identified. In contrast, international cases reveal spatial strategies that promote free play, autonomy, and connection with nature. Finally, five design dimensions are proposed to guide the redesign of these spaces as inclusive, active, and enriching environments for vulnerable children and their personal development","childcare services; child development; landscape architecture; learning environments; outdoor spaces · Fiorella Margarita Mechato-Lara (Bogotá) VOL 28 · Nº 2 · JULIO-DICIEMBRE 2026 · ISSN: 1657-0308 · E-ISSN: 2357-626X CAR (Centro de Acogida Residencial): “Espacio físico administrado por una institución pública, privada, mixta o comunal donde viven niñas, niños y adolescentes en situación de a",marcadores,1.0.0,21e89d6f35660c21
2357-626X,3-6931-Residenando+aldeas_2026.pdf,3-6931-Residenando+aldeas_2026_0,en,"en Europa, Asia y América Latina. El desarrollo del trabajo comprendió tres fases: diagnóstico","* Arquitecta, Universidad Nacional de Ingeniería. Lima (Perú). Filiación institucional: Universidad Nacional de Ingeniería, Facultad de Arquitectura, Urbanismo y Artes. Lima (Perú). https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=es&authuser=1&user=ysHX0usAAAAJ https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2748-8627 fmechatol@uni.pe / fiorella0301@gmail.com Thousands of children and adolescents in situations of abandonment live in infrastructures that, while functional, do not promote their overall well-being or emotional development. In this context, the present article reflects on the architecture of children’s villages and similar typologies in Peru, proposing a typological reconfiguration with an architectural-landscape approach grounded in pedagogical principles. The aim is to identify design criteria that transform these spaces into educational, stimulating, and well-being-oriented environments. The literature review encompassed approaches such as neuroarchitecture, Montessori, Waldorf, and Patio Vivo pedagogies, as well as national and international regulatory guidelines related to the typology under study. The research adopted a qualitative exploratory approach, based on the analysis of multiple cases, combining indirect observation (Google Maps, plans, photographs) with international references in Europe, Asia, and Latin America. The study comprised three phases: diagnosis of Peruvian children’s villages, review of global references, and formulation of spatial guidelines. Among the findings, an underuse of outdoor areas, limited diversity of uses, and poor landscape integration were identified. In contrast, international cases reveal spatial strategies that promote free play, autonomy, and connection with nature. Finally, five design dimensions are proposed to guide the redesign of these spaces as inclusive, active, and enriching environments for vulnerable children and their personal development","childcare services; child development; landscape architecture; learning environments; outdoor spaces · Fiorella Margarita Mechato-Lara (Bogotá) VOL 28 · Nº 2 · JULIO-DICIEMBRE 2026 · ISSN: 1657-0308 · E-ISSN: 2357-626X CAR (Centro de Acogida Residencial): “Espacio físico administrado por una institución pública, privada, mixta o comunal donde viven niñas, niños y adolescentes en situación de a",marcadores,1.0.0,1200f0619ddcdd91
2357-626X,4-4437-CirculacionBrasilGuay-2026.pdf,4-4437-CirculacionBrasilGuay-2026_0,es,Circulación de ideas y relaciones formales entre,"Este estudio sobre arquitecturas latinoamericanas de Brasil y Ecuador analiza la circulación de ideas y las relaciones formales entre sus producciones modernas, con el objetivo de identificar los mecanismos de transferencia y reinterpretación del lenguaje arquitectónico en la ciudad de Guayaquil. Con base en la teoría de la circulación internacional de las ideas, particularmente entre p aíses en desarrollo, se p lantea un análisis comparativo entre obras construidas en Brasil y edificaciones guayaquileñas erigidas entre 1950 y 1980. El estudio examina las correspondencias y divergencias formales, materiales y conceptuales que muestran la apropiación y resignificación de r eferentes del movimiento moderno brasileño en el contexto ecuatoriano. Los resultados muestran c ómo estas prácticas proyectuales contribuyeron a configurar una identidad arquitectónica local vinculada a los procesos de modernización latinoamericanos","América Latina; análisis comparativo; arquitectura moderna, identidad cultural; patrimonio cultural | 75 76 | REVISTA DE (BOGOTÁ)",marcadores,1.0.0,5a04c6cd668c6368
2357-626X,4-4437-CirculacionBrasilGuay-2026.pdf,4-4437-CirculacionBrasilGuay-2026_0,en,s mecanismos de transferencia y reinterpretación del lenguaje arquitectónico en la ciudad de,"* ** Arquitecto, Universidad de Guayaquil (Ecuador). Doctor en Gestión Urbana, Pontificia Universidad Católica de Paraná (PUCPR). Curitiba (Brasil). Filiación institucional: Universidad de Guayaquil (Ecuador). https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=es&user=ZWiGcxsAAAAJ https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2883-4715 david.hidalgos@ug.edu.ec / davidhidalgourbanista@gmail.com Arquitecta y urbanista, Pontificia Universidad Católica de Paraná (PUCPR). Curitiba (Brasil). Maestría y doctorado en Gestión Urbana, Pontificia Universidad Católica de Paraná (PUCPR). Curitiba (Brasil). Filiación institucional: Pontificia Universidad Católica de Paraná (PUCPR). Curitiba (Brasil). https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=WIEq-IcAAAAJ&hl=pt-BR. https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7627-8346 manoela.mjazar@pucpr.br / manoelamj.arq@gmail.com This study of Latin American architecture in Brazil and Ecuador analyzes the circulation of ideas and the formal relationships between their modern architectural production, with the aim of identifying the mechanisms of transfer and reinterpretation of architectural language in the city of Guayaquil. Based on the theory of international circulation of ideas, particularly among developing countries, a comparative analysis is proposed of buildings constructed in Brazil and structures erected in Guayaquil between 1950 and 1980. The study examines the formal, material, and conceptual correspondences and divergences that reveal the appropriation and resignification of references from the Brazilian Modern Movement within the Ecuadorian context. The findings show how these design practices contributed to shaping a local architectural identity linked to Latin American modernization processes",comparative analysis; cultural identity; cultural heritage; Latin America; modern architecture David 